6. FIX FINALITY, STORAGE ATOMICITY AND CONSENSUS COMMITMENT

Treat this as the highest security priority.

The Network must never announce a transaction as final when its authoritative committed data has not been successfully persisted.

The required logical sequence is:

Receive operation
       |
       v
Validate operation
       |
       v
Verify participant authorization
       |
       v
Verify protocol rules
       |
       v
Construct deterministic proposal
       |
       v
Independently validate proposal
       |
       v
Obtain valid consensus certificate
       |
       v
Prepare durable commit
       |
       v
Verify storage success
       |
       v
Commit authoritative state atomically
       |
       v
Publish committed result
       |
       v
Notify peers

Correct the actual ordering in the code.

Never discard storage errors.

Never use ignored storage results in protocol-critical paths.

Never update externally observable committed state before the required durable commit.

Never broadcast finality before the commit process has succeeded according to the storage and consensus design.

Durable atomic persistence

Introduce a genuine transactional storage design.

It must safely persist all related data, including:

* Proposal.
* Event history.
* Consensus certificate.
* Resulting state.
* State root.
* Ledger commitments.
* Transaction status.
* Entity indices.
* Program execution records.
* Relevant validator metadata.

Use a real transaction or a carefully designed journal with explicit commit records and crash recovery.

Sequential independent writes to several files are not sufficient evidence of an atomic transaction.

A crash between any two persistence steps must not create an ambiguous authoritative state.

Support:

* Transaction IDs.
* Idempotent retries.
* Duplicate detection.
* Durable commit markers.
* Corruption detection.
* Recovery.
* Snapshot verification.
* Storage migration.
* Database consistency checks.

