Membership rules

Validator membership must itself be authorized and committed through protocol rules.

A single local method must not allow an operator to insert an arbitrary validator into the authoritative set.

Use canonical validator-set hashes or equivalent cryptographic commitments.

Every proposal and vote must bind to the applicable validator-set epoch.

Do not use a permanently fixed epoch when membership can change.

Ensure that the Network’s canonical genesis hash commits to the actual genesis configuration, including the appropriate initial validator information.

Nodes must reject an unexpected genesis configuration.

Consensus assumptions

Document the precise fault model, safety assumptions, liveness assumptions, quorum thresholds and network-partition behaviour.

A BFT configuration of 3f + 1 validators and 2f + 1 approvals is only justified when the complete protocol and membership assumptions support it.

Do not simply implement a percentage threshold and claim Byzantine fault tolerance.

Independent verification

Every validator participating in a proposal must independently verify:

* Program.
* Authorization.
* Network identity.
* Validator membership.
* Operation scope.
* Token invariants.
* Resulting state.
* Resulting state root.

A participant signature is not consensus.

A hash is not authorization.

A quorum certificate is not proof of Wallet ownership.

Keep these responsibilities separate.

